<body>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anand Patel
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate two families of divisors which we expect to play a distinguished role in the global geometry of Hurwitz space. In particular, we show that they are extremal and rigid in the small degree regime <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. We further show their significance in the problem of computing the sweeping slope of Hurwitz space in these degrees. In the process, we prove various general results about the divisor theory of Hurwitz space, including a proof of the independence of the boundary components of the admissible covers compactification. Some basic open questions and further directions are discussed at the end.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S0.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">0.1 Introduction.</h3>

<div id="S0.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hurwitz space, the parameter space of degree <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> genus <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> branched covers of <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, has been studied by many mathematicians for over a century. One of the main threads in this long history concerns the “Hurwitz number problem” which asks to enumerate the number of branched covers with prescribed ramification behaviour above fixed points in <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Along similar lines, much effort has gone into understanding topological properties of the “boundary" of Hurwitz space – by this we mean the locus of covers with non-simple branching or small Galois group. This “boundary” is naturally stratified, and many have studied this stratification. One of the main motivating problems here is to classify irreducible components of the stratification. (The list of results devoted to these topics is so long that the author would rather not cite anyone for fear of leaving someone out.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter spaces of branched covers have also played various important auxiliary roles in the study of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of curves. The first instance of this is the theorem typically attributed to Clebsch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cle73</a>]</cite> which says that Hurwitz space is irreducible, implying the irreducibility of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Diaz’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dia84</a>]</cite>, which gives a nontrivial upper bound on the dimension of a proper subvariety of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, makes essential use of Hurwitz spaces. The celebrated theorem on the Kodaira dimension of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM82</a>]</cite> uses the divisor class expression for the locus of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal curves, with <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\lfloor g/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\lfloor g/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> odd. Many relations in the tautological ring of <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> have been found using an alternate compactification of the space of branched covers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">PP13</a>]</cite>. The point here is that spaces of branched covers occur as central tools in the study of curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">What is lacking is a study of Hurwitz space from a more algebro-geometric and less combinatorial or topological point of view, especially a study of subvarieties parametrizing branched covers with special algebro-geometric properties; properties not captured by degenerate branching behaviour or Galois group. The literature here is much more sparse, and our intention in this paper is to introduce the special role that certain subvarieties play in the algebraic geometry of Hurwitz space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary focus in this paper is to study particular codimension one loci in the <span id="S0.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">interior</span> of Hurwitz spaces. These subvarieties are called the <span id="S0.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni</span> and <span id="S0.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Casnati-Ekedahl</span> divisors, and our goal is to explain their distinguished role in the divisor theory of Hurwitz spaces of low degree. They arise as a “cohomology jump” phenomenon, and are similar in spirit to the “Koszul divisors” of G. Farkas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Far09</a>]</cite>. It should be emphasized that the Maroni and Casnati-Ekedahl divisors have very little to do with branching behavior or Galois groups of covers – they arise from considering finer algebraic invariants of a cover, generalizing the classical <span id="S0.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni invariant</span> of trigonal curves first discovered by Maroni in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mar46</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S0.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">0.2 Outline and main results.</h3>

<div id="S0.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S1" title="1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section 1</span></a>, we define the Maroni and the Casnati-Ekedahl divisors, denoted by <math id="S0.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and briefly introduce the reader to a broader context in which they arise. In particular, we call attention to a very ill-understood stratification of Hurwitz space arising from a well-known general structure theorem due to Casnati and Ekedahl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, in <a href="#S2" title="2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section 2</span></a>, we abruptly change topic and prove a basic theorem about the boundary components of the admissible cover compactification <math id="S0.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm adm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm adm}</annotation></semantics></math> and its “normalization,” the space of twisted stable maps <math id="S0.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main result we show is:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Independence of the boundary)<span id="Thmmaintheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The divisor classes of boundary components are independent in <math id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Pic}\,\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\otimes{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Pic}\,\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\otimes{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the concept of a <span id="S0.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">partial pencil</span>, a particular type of one parameter family of admissible covers which will be used heavily in the later parts of the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In section <a href="#S3" title="3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section 3</span></a>, we obtain partial expressions for the divisor class expressions of <math id="S0.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of some natural divisor classes on Hurwitz space. We also recall the classical unirational parametrizations of Hurwitz space when <math id="S0.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. Using these parametrizations, we are able to show:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Rigidity and Extremality)<span id="Thmmaintheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Apart from one exception, the Maroni and Casnati-Ekedahl divisors are rigid and extremal in <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. The exception is <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the Casnati-Ekedahl divisor has two irreducible components, each of which is rigid and extremal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We view this result as a first validation of our basic claim that these divisors play a distinguished role in the geometry of <math id="S0.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We end the section by indicating how one might hope to tackle the question of rigidity or extremality for higher degrees <math id="S0.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section 4</span></a> is the most technical part of the paper. Its purpose is to demonstrate the role that <math id="S0.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> play in the well-known problem of determining a sharp upper bound for the <span id="S0.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">slope</span> of a “sweeping” complete one parameter family <math id="S0.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the slope <math id="S0.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is the ratio <math id="S0.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\cdot B/\lambda\cdot B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\cdot B/\lambda\cdot B</annotation></semantics></math>, and we define</p>
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}):=\sup_{\text{$B\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}$ sweeping}}s(B)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext> sweeping</mtext></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}):=\sup_{\text{$B\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}$ sweeping}}s(B).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Cornalba and Harris <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH88</a>]</cite> show that <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{2,g})=8+4/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{2,g})=8+4/g</annotation></semantics></math>, while Stankova <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">SF00</a>]</cite> shows that <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+6/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+6/g</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Deopurkar and the author <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP12</a>]</cite> completed the trigonal case by showing that <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+20/(3g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+20/(3g+1)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
Beorchia and Zucconi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BZ12</a>]</cite> consider slopes of a large class of one parameter families in <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and produce slope bounds for families in this large class. Their focus is not on sweeping families – in fact they consider a larger class of curves and therefore their bounds are different from the number <math id="S0.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this section is:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Slope bounds)<span id="Thmmaintheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S0.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S0.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Stankova </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">SF00</a><span id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) Let </span><math id="S0.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be even. Then</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+\frac{6}{g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{3,g})=7+\frac{6}{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S0.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S0.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(P –) Let </span><math id="S0.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3\mod 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3\mod 6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g})=\frac{13}{2}+\frac{15}{2g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>13</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>15</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{4,g})=\frac{13}{2}+\frac{15}{2g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S0.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S0.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(P –) Let </span><math id="S0.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=16\mod 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>mod</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=16\mod 20</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then</span></p>
<table id="S0.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{5,g})=\frac{31}{5}+\frac{44}{5g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>31</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>44</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{5,g})=\frac{31}{5}+\frac{44}{5g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In all three cases there exist sweeping families achieving the given bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As a consequence of this theorem, we also deduce the same slope bounds for sweeping families in the corresponding <math id="S0.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal locus <math id="S0.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{d,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{d,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S0.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 0.1</span></span><span id="S0.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S0.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for the slightly different compactifications <math id="S0.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same proof method applies for <math id="S0.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g},</annotation></semantics></math> as we explain in proof <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS7" title="4.5.7 The proof of  and . ‣ 4.5 A proposition on “rotating directrices.” ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S0.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">0.3 Acknowledgements.</h3>

<div id="S0.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I am grateful to Joe Harris for introducing me to these topics. I thank Gabriel Bujokas and Anand Deopurkar for uncountably many useful conversations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S0.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">0.4 Notation and conventions.</h3>

<div id="S0.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work over an algebraically closed field <math id="S0.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic <math id="S0.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. All stacks will be Deligne-Mumford stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S0.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally free sheaf, then <math id="S0.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the projective bundle <math id="S0.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{\rm Proj}\,}({\rm Sym}^{\bullet}{\mathcal{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Proj</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{\rm Proj}\,}({\rm Sym}^{\bullet}{\mathcal{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S0.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the divisor class associated to the natural <math id="S0.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A subscript under <math id="S0.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g. <math id="S0.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, will be used to distinguish it from other rational curves being discussed. <math id="S0.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> will denote a coordinate on <math id="S0.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Invariants of a branched cover.</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.1 The Maroni invariants and Tschirnhausen bundle.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A branched cover <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">relative canonical embedding</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i{\colon}C\hookrightarrow{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i{\colon}C\hookrightarrow{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle. The scroll <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as the set of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes spanned by the degree <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in the canonical space of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. (This geometric realization makes sense when <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;g</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is described by a linear family of degree <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors on the curve <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. we have an inclusion <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}\subset{\rm Sym}^{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}\subset{\rm Sym}^{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. The projectivized normal bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}(N_{{\bf P}^{1}/{\rm Sym}^{d}(C)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}(N_{{\bf P}^{1}/{\rm Sym}^{d}(C)})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the scroll <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> mentioned earlier. (This description makes sense for all <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,g</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, there is a purely algebraic definition of the vector bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> which was implicitly mentioned above. The map <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives an exact sequence in which the dual of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> sits as the cokernel:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\longrightarrow{{\mathcal{E}}}^{\vee}\longrightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\longrightarrow{{\mathcal{E}}}^{\vee}\longrightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{{\bf P}^{1}}(-,{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{{\bf P}^{1}}(-,{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.3 ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}}\longrightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}^{1}}\longrightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> on the curve <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, which by a theorem of Casnati and Ekedahl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>[Theorem 2.1] induces the relative canonical map <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>. From this definition, it is easy to see that the degree of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, while the rank of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is clearly <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The vector bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Tschirnhausen bundle</span> of the cover <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and its splitting type can be viewed as a set of discrete invariants for a branched cover. These invariants are called the <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">scrollar invariants</span> or <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni invariants</span>, and it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cop99</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal03</a>]</cite> that for a Zariski open subset of Hurwitz space, the splitting type of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">balanced</span>, i.e. <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{E}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{E}},{\mathcal{E}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the case <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the scrollar invariant is simply the classical Maroni invariant; this explains the following definition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A branched cover <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni-special</span> if its Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not balanced. We let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed substack parametrizing Maroni-special curves.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Deopurkar and the author prove in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite>[Theorem 2.10] that the locus <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension one only when the divisibility condition <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)\mid g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)\mid g</annotation></semantics></math> holds, in which case the locus is nonempty and irreducible. It is an interesting open problem, in these cases, to determine if <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid or extremal as an effective divisor. <a href="#Thmmaintheorem2" title="Theorem B (Rigidity and Extremality). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> establishes these properties of <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> for branched covers of degrees <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The scrollar invariants have certainly appeared before in the literature, most notably in the works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal03</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cop99</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch86</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a>]</cite>. However, the study of the geometry of the locus of Maroni-special curves has only recently attracted attention – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite> for more on this topic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The splitting type of the vector bundle <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes, and is determined by, the dimensions of the powers of the <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the map <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.2 The Casnati-Ekedahl invariants and the bundle of quadrics.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are more discrete invariants coming from the geometry of the relative canonical embedding which are far less understood, and they arise as the splitting type of a different vector bundle. Part of our aim in this paper is to highlight their significance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fiber of the projection <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}({\mathcal{E}})\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The general structure theorem for branched covers, proved by Casnati and Ekedahl in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>, shows that the degree <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisor <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}(t)\subset{\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}(t)\subset{\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arithmetically Gorenstein subscheme for all <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this implies that the vector spaces</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{t}:=\{\text{Quadrics in ${\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}$ containing $\alpha^{-1}(t)$}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Quadrics in </mtext><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mtext> containing </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{t}:=\{\text{Quadrics in ${\bf P}^{d-2}_{t}$ containing $\alpha^{-1}(t)$}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">have the same rank, independent of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore glue together to form a vector bundle <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we call the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">bundle of quadrics</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Algebraically, the bundle <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized easily via its relationship with the Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, from <a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1.3 ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, we conclude that there is a map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Sym}^{2}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Sym}^{2}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is easily seen to be surjective. The bundle <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the kernel of this map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are not aware of a nice description of the bundle <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the geometry of the singularity <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in W_{d}\subset{\rm Pic}\,^{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in W_{d}\subset{\rm Pic}\,^{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, as we had for <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The splitting type of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides an additional discrete invariant of a branched cover which is essentially “independent” of the scrollar invariants, in the sense that one set of invariants does not determine the other. The isomorphism class of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a generic curve is understood under the provision that <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>: The Main Theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite> states that <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced for a Zariski open set of covers, i.e. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{F}},{\mathcal{F}})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{F}},{\mathcal{F}})=0.</annotation></semantics></math> In complete analogy with <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1.1 The Maroni invariants and Tschirnhausen bundle. ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we make the following definition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say a cover <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">-special</span> if its bundle of quadrics <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not balanced, i.e. <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{F}},{\mathcal{F}})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ext\,}^{1}({\mathcal{F}},{\mathcal{F}})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed substack parametrizing <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>-special covers.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We have not fully classified the circumstances under which the locus <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is divisorial, but the natural expectation is that this occurs precisely when the rank <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>rk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> divides the degree <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This translates into the divisibility condition <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(d-3)/2\mid(d-3)(g+d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(d-3)/2\mid(d-3)(g+d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and it follows from the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite> that for such pairs <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,d)</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>-locus is an effective divisor. As in the case of <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is an interesting problem to establish in these instances whether the divisor <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid or extremal – we establish this when <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#Thmmaintheorem2" title="Theorem B (Rigidity and Extremality). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. As we discuss in the last section, the Casnati-Ekedahl divisor may lose some rigidity properties when <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, although we cannot prove this.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When we refer to the divisor <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> in a compactification of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> we always take the <span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">closure</span> of the respective loci in the interior.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.3 </h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bundles <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> are two bundles among a whole host of bundles naturally associated to a branched cover, as explained by a theorem of Casnati and Ekedahl. We review this fundamental theorem here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be integral schemes and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> a finite flat Gorenstein morphism of degree <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\longrightarrow{{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\longrightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\longrightarrow{{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\longrightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}={\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}={\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="S1.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tschirnhausen bundle</em> of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{Y}(-,{\mathcal{O}}_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{Y}(-,{\mathcal{O}}_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.3 ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}\longrightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}\longrightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 2.1]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
In the setting above, <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> gives an embedding <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\pi\circ\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\pi\circ\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection. Moreover, the subscheme <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as follows.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The resolution of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module has the form</span></p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}(-d)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-3}(-d+2)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-4}(-d+3)\longrightarrow\dots{\\
}\dots\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{2}(-3)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{1}(-2)\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\longrightarrow 0,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mi>
</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}(-d)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-3}(-d+2)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{d-4}(-d+3)\longrightarrow\dots{\\
}\dots\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{2}(-3)\longrightarrow\pi^{*}{\mathcal{N}}_{1}(-2)\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\longrightarrow 0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are vector bundles on </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Restricted to a point </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, this sequence is the minimal free resolution of length </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, zero dimensional scheme </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}_{y}\cong{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}_{y}\cong{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ranks of the </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are given by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{N}}_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}{d\choose{i+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">rk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{N}}_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}{d\choose{i+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}\cong\pi^{*}\det{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}\cong\pi^{*}\det{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Furthermore, the resolution is symmetric, that is, isomorphic to the resolution obtained by applying </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hom_{{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}}(-,{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}(-d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hom_{{\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}}(-,{\mathcal{N}}_{d-2}(-d))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The branch divisor of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a section of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\det{\mathcal{E}})^{\otimes 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\det{\mathcal{E}})^{\otimes 2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of (arithmetic) genus <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y={\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y={\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{E}}=d-1\,\,\text{and }\deg{\mathcal{E}}=g+d-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>rk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{E}}=d-1\,\,\text{and }\deg{\mathcal{E}}=g+d-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.4 </h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By considering a particular bundle <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> occurring in the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In item 1 ‣ Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1.3 ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>), one can consider the loci in Hurwitz space parametrizing covers whose bundle <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a prescribed splitting type. In this way, Hurwitz space becomes stratified by many algebro-geometric invariants. This stratification is currently very mysterious – in this paper we focus only on the invariants associated to the bundles <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}:={\mathcal{N}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}:={\mathcal{N}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.5 The Maroni loci <math id="S1.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hurwitz space <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> contains natural subvarieties (or substacks) which we call the <span id="S1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni loci</span> <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, we make the following definition:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, degree <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+d-1</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle on <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <span id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Maroni locus</span> <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}):=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{E}}\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}):=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{E}}\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly if <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty then <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg{\mathcal{E}}=g+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg{\mathcal{E}}=g+d-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{E}}=d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>rk</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{rk}\,}{\mathcal{E}}=d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. However, more can be said about <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> in this situation. For instance, <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have any nonpositive summands, since this would force <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to be disconnected. Write</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}={\mathcal{O}}(a_{1})\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(a_{2})...\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(a_{d-1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}={\mathcal{O}}(a_{1})\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(a_{2})...\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(a_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq...\leq a_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq...\leq a_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. and set <math id="S1.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor:=a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor:=a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We maintain the notation above. A vector bundle <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+d-1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">tame</span> if <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}-a_{i-1}\leq\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}-a_{i-1}\leq\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,...,d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,...,d-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among all tame bundles <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor=m</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a most generic one, which we call <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the unique tame bundle which maximizes the sum <math id="S1.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)a_{1}+(d-2)a_{2}+...+a_{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)a_{1}+(d-2)a_{2}+...+a_{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The most comprehensive theorem on the geometry of the Maroni loci to date is found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite>, which relies heavily on the work of Ohbuchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a>]</cite> and Coppens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cop99</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deopurkar, P- <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying <math id="S1.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nonempty, then </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a tame bundle.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor{\mathcal{E}}\rfloor\leq m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> then </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})\subset{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}})\subset{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])\subset{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m+1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])\subset{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m+1])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an irreducible subvariety of </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of codimension </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-(d-1)m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-(d-1)m+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> unless </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, in which case </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])={\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}[m])={\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, point <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4)</annotation></semantics></math> says that a general cover <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has a balanced Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S1.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">1.5.1 The Maroni divisor <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}\subset{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Item <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S1.Thmthm9" title="Theorem 1.9 (Deopurkar, P- []). ‣ 1.5 The Maroni loci 
          
            
              M
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a> gives a complete description of all divisorial Maroni loci. Setting the codimension <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-(d-1)m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-(d-1)m+1</annotation></semantics></math> equal to <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> gives the divisibility relation</p>
<table id="S1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)\mid g." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)\mid g.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">When this congruence condition is met, the general Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}^{gen}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}^{gen}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">perfectly</span> balanced, i.e.</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}^{gen}={\mathcal{O}}(k)^{\oplus d-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}^{gen}={\mathcal{O}}(k)^{\oplus d-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=g/(d-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=g/(d-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the special bundle</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}^{sp}:={\mathcal{O}}(k-1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(k)^{d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(k+1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}^{sp}:={\mathcal{O}}(k-1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(k)^{d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(k+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Then the Maroni locus <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}^{sp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}({\mathcal{E}}^{sp})</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the locus <math id="S1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Definition 1.1. ‣ 1.1 The Maroni invariants and Tschirnhausen bundle. ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci <math id="S1.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now shift our attention to the bundle of quadrics <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a cover <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}.</annotation></semantics></math> As in <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Definition 1.7. ‣ 1.5 The Maroni loci 
          
            
              M
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>, we can consider the loci in <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the bundle <math id="S1.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a rank <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(d-3)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(d-3)/2</annotation></semantics></math>, degree <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-3)(g+d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-3)(g+d-1)</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundle on <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <span id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Casnati-Ekedahl locus</span> <math id="S1.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}}):=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{F}}\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}}):=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{F}}\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Very little is known about the Casnati-Ekedahl loci <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{C}}({\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> - a general theorem analogous to part <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S1.Thmthm9" title="Theorem 1.9 (Deopurkar, P- []). ‣ 1.5 The Maroni loci 
          
            
              M
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.9</span></a> has only recently been shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite> using a somewhat delicate degeneration argument. We provide here a simpler proof in the case <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> because we will need to refer to the method of proof later. We note that Bopp proves the case of degree <math id="S1.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> (the only interesting case) using very different methods in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bop14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.11</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S1.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced for a general cover <math id="S1.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will use a degeneration argument. To begin, we establish the proposition for <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> by a case by case analysis. We also record the genus <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> cases for arbitrary <math id="S1.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.12</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon R\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon R\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be degree </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> covers where </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a smooth rational curve and </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a smooth elliptic curve. Then the following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I3.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus{d-2\choose 2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus{d-2\choose 2}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-2}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}={\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-2}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(d)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}={\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus\frac{d(d-3)}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}={\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus\frac{d(d-3)}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a general degree </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> cover, with </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(C)\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(C)\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is balanced.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 1.12. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.12</span></a>:</h6>
<div id="S1.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simply check all cases:</p>
<ol id="S1.I4.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All summands of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive and add up to <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, all summands have degree <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the relative canonical factorization <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota{\colon}R\longrightarrow{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota{\colon}R\longrightarrow{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may think of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> as lying inside <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The series <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\zeta-f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\zeta-f|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the complete series <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(d-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(d-2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the projection morphism <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q{\colon}{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q{\colon}{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the series <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\zeta-f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\zeta-f|</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the embedding of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a rational normal curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The rational normal curve <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{d-2\choose 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{d-2\choose 2}</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional space of quadrics. The class of these quadrics, when pulled back along the projection <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, is <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\zeta-2f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\zeta-2f</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall the exact sequence on the target <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}({\omega^{\otimes 2}_{\alpha}})\longrightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}({\omega^{\otimes 2}_{\alpha}})\longrightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We twist by <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(-2)</annotation></semantics></math> and consider global sections. The previous paragraph along with projective normality of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> imply that <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}({\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}(-2))={d-2\choose 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}({\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}(-2))={d-2\choose 2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude by noting that (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In item 1b ‣ item 1 ‣ Proof of : ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.8</span></a>) shows that no summand of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> may exceed <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the degree of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> must be <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-3)(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-3)(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This forces the splitting type of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus{d-2\choose 2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus{d-2\choose 2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All summands of <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive, and their degrees sum to <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> must be as indicated.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(d)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Analogous to part <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we give a seperate proof which will be used later in <a href="#S4.Thmthm30" title="Proposition 4.30. ‣ 4.4.7 The partial pencil 
            
              p
            
           avoids 
            
              CE
            
          . ‣ 4.4 Degree five analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>. The elliptic curve <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math> via the complete series <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\beta^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\beta^{*}{\mathcal{O}}(1)|</annotation></semantics></math> as an elliptic normal curve, and the map <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a pencil of hyperplane sections <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(s)=H_{s}\cap X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(s)=H_{s}\cap X</annotation></semantics></math>. For each hyperplane section <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-1}(s)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-1}(s),</annotation></semantics></math> it is well known that the restriction map</p>
<table id="S1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm res}_{s}{\colon}\{\text{Quadrics containing $X$}\}\longrightarrow\{\text{Quadrics in $H_{s}$ containing $\beta^{-1}(s)$}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>res</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Quadrics containing </mtext><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Quadrics in </mtext><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mtext> containing </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm res}_{s}{\colon}\{\text{Quadrics containing $X$}\}\longrightarrow\{\text{Quadrics in $H_{s}$ containing $\beta^{-1}(s)$}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is an isomorphism. (Note that no quadric containing <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> may contain a hyperplane <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the domain of this restriction map is independent of the parameter <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> must be <span id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">perfectly</span> balanced. Then, for degree reasons, we conclude <math id="S1.I4.i1.I1.i4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again, we check all cases. We need only consider <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>, as these are the only instances when <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists at all. We label each case by the pair <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, furthermore, we always assume <math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is general in moduli.</p>
<ol id="S1.I4.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The degree of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, and since it is a subbundle of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2))</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that either <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}={\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}={\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}={\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}={\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>. The former cannot happen. Indeed, the curve <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> would then be a complete intersection of two divisors <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\in|2\zeta-f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\in|2\zeta-f|</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}\in|2\zeta-4f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}\in|2\zeta-4f|</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter linear system consists of one non reduced divisor, so <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> would not be reduced. Therefore, <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,5)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The degree of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>, and its rank is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subbundle of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus 2}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)^{\oplus 2}\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which means the largest potential degree of a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it cannot happen that <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> is a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>: The sections of the linear system <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|2\zeta-4f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|2\zeta-4f|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">reducible</span>, and no such reducible section can contain <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the non-degeneracy of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (<math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not lie in a sub scroll.) Therefore, the largest degree of a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we argue that <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot split as <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(3)^{\oplus 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(3)^{\oplus 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the only potential non-balanced possibility. This follows from the fact that the restriction map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(2\zeta-3f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C}+D))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(2\zeta-3f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C}+D))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is surjective. Here <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the divisor class of the <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The surjectivity of restriction map, in turn, follows from the following easy observations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">i.</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction maps</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(\zeta-2f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(\zeta-2f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(\zeta-f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}+D))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(\zeta-f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}+D))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">are surjective.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">ii.</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The multiplication map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}))\otimes H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}+D))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C}+D))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}))\otimes H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(\omega_{C}+D))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C}+D))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is surjective.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis is completely similar to the <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math> case.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,5)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The degree of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, and its rank is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subbundle of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 3})</annotation></semantics></math>, which means the largest potential degree of a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is again <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as before, it cannot happen that <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> is a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>: The sections of the linear system <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|2\zeta-4f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|2\zeta-4f|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> are sums of products of sections of the linear system <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\zeta-2f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\zeta-2f|</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the same is true about the linear system <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|2\omega_{C}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|2\omega_{C}|</annotation></semantics></math>: all sections are sums of products of sections of the canonical series. This is to say that the restriction map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(2\zeta-4f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{\colon}H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}}(2\zeta-4f))\longrightarrow H^{0}({\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2\omega_{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is an isomorphism, which implies that <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot have <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> as a summand. The degree constraint on <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> then forces it to be balanced.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis is analogous to the <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math> case, so we skip it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,5)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The degree of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, and its rank is <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subbundle of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}{\mathcal{E}}=S^{2}({\mathcal{O}}(1)\oplus{\mathcal{O}}(2)^{\oplus 3})</annotation></semantics></math>, which means the largest potential degree of a summand of <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is again <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. We now show that there is a unique <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> summand in <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reader can easily check that this follows from the fact that <math id="S1.I4.i2.I1.ix6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a unique quadric in its canonical embedding.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a>:</h6>
<div id="S1.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a><span id="S1.SS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text"> follows from <a href="#S1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 1.12. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.12</span></a>, by a degeneration argument. Indeed, suppose we begin with a general cover <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose we know <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a> holds for <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will show then, that <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a> holds for <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g+d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g+d}</annotation></semantics></math> as well. Pick a general fiber <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and attach an elliptic cover <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{\colon}X\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting admissible cover <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}({\rm End\,}{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}({\rm End\,}{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha})=0</annotation></semantics></math> - this follows from the fact that <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is perfectly balanced, as shown in <a href="#S1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 1.12. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.12</span></a>. Therefore, by smoothing <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> and appealing to upper semi-continuity, we obtain covers in <math id="S1.SS6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g+d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g+d}</annotation></semantics></math> which have balanced bundles of quadrics.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The second part of <a href="#S1.Thmthm12" title="Lemma 1.12. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.12</span></a> then provides the base cases of the induction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.13</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reader may wonder, given the existence of explicit classical constructions for all covers of degree <math id="S1.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, why we choose to prove <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a> via degeneration rather than constructively. Certainly it is possible (and simpler) to prove <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Proposition 1.11. ‣ 1.6 The Casnati-Ekedahl loci 
          
            
              C
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a> constructively using Bertini-type theorems, however we will need to refer to the method of degeneration in the above proof later (e.g. <a href="#S4.Thmthm30" title="Proposition 4.30. ‣ 4.4.7 The partial pencil 
            
              p
            
           avoids 
            
              CE
            
          . ‣ 4.4 Degree five analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.30</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">1.7 Relating <math id="S1.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</h3>

<div id="S1.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We record the following fact conjectured by Casnati in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas99</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.14</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Conjecture <math id="S1.Thmthm14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas99</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any finite, flat map between smooth varieties <math id="S1.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{F}})=(d-3)c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{F}})=(d-3)c_{1}({\mathcal{E}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We establish the slightly stronger identity</span></p>
<table id="S1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N}))=(2N-1)c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N}))=(2N-1)c_{1}({\mathcal{E}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">from which the proposition will follow when <math id="S1.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The identity <a href="#S1.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 1.7 Relating 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a> is proved by a straightforward application of the Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch formula to the map <math id="S1.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\longrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> and the sheaf <math id="S1.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\otimes N}_{\alpha}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\otimes N}_{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math> Indeed, if we consider the degree one part of the equality</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ch[\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N})]\cdot Td(T_{Y})=\alpha_{*}[ch(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N})\cdot Td(T_{X})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ch[\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N})]\cdot Td(T_{Y})=\alpha_{*}[ch(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N})\cdot Td(T_{X})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS7.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and use the fact that <math id="S1.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the class of the branch divisor of <math id="S1.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, we arrive at the equality</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N}))-\frac{d}{2}K_{Y}=\alpha_{*}[Nc_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})-\frac{K_{X}}{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes N}))-\frac{d}{2}K_{Y}=\alpha_{*}[Nc_{1}(\omega_{\alpha})-\frac{K_{X}}{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS7.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">which, when rearranged, gives (<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 1.7 Relating 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a>) because <math id="S1.SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{*}(\frac{K_{X}}{2})-\frac{d}{2}K_{Y}=c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{*}(\frac{K_{X}}{2})-\frac{d}{2}K_{Y}=c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.Thmthm14" title="Proposition 1.14 (Conjecture 
            
              2.5
            
           in []). ‣ 1.7 Relating 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.14</span></a><span id="S1.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">follows by using (<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 1.7 Relating 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  E
                  )
                
              
            
          
         and 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                c
                1
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  F
                  )
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a>) when <math id="S1.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and by considering using the exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\rm Sym}^{2}{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})\longrightarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{F}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow{\rm Sym}^{2}{\mathcal{E}}_{\alpha}\longrightarrow\alpha_{*}(\omega_{\alpha}^{\otimes 2})\longrightarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.15</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With more care, the argument above can be made to show that the first Chern classes of all syzygy bundles <math id="S1.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> occuring in the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution <a href="#S1.E5" title="In item 1 ‣ Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1.3 ‣ 1 Invariants of a branched cover. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> are multiples of <math id="S1.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>. With even more care, one can deduce many relationships among the total Chern characters of the bundles <math id="S1.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Compactifications.</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.1 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will work with a number of spaces and compactifications – the definitions follow. Note that we do <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">not</span> label ramification points in our compactifications of Hurwitz space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S2.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the moduli space parametrizing smooth, proper, genus <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the Deligne-Mumford compactification of <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the stack parametrizing <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve of genus <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> a finite map of degree <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with simple branching (that is, the branch divisor of <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is supported at <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points). Two such covers <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> are considered isomorphic if there are isomorphisms <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi{\colon}C_{1}\longrightarrow C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi{\colon}C_{1}\longrightarrow C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\psi\circ\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\psi\circ\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the stack parametrizing <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an at-worst-nodal curve of arithmetic genus <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> a finite map of degree <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The notion of equivalence is the same as that for <math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. This space allows arbitrary branching behavior.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix5" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{{\rm ns}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>ns</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{{\rm ns}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the stack parametrizing <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an at-worst-nodal curve of arithmetic genus <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">with only non-separating nodes</span>, and <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> a finite map of degree <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The notion of equivalence is the same as that for <math id="S2.I1.ix5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. This space allows arbitrary branching behavior.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix6" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the Harris-Mumford admissible cover compactification of <math id="S2.I1.ix6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">HM82</a>]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix7" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the compactification of <math id="S2.I1.ix7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by twisted stable maps, as defined by Abramovich, Corti, and Vistoli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACV03</a>]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix8" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the alternate compactification of <math id="S2.I1.ix8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by twisted stable maps where at most <math id="S2.I1.ix8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> branch points are allowed to collide at a time. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deo13</a>]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix9" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(3)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the alternate compactification of <math id="S2.I1.ix9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by twisted stable maps where at most <math id="S2.I1.ix9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> branch points are allowed to collide at a time. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deo13</a>]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S2.I1.ix10" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{1}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{1}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S2.I1.ix10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the locus in <math id="S2.I1.ix10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing <math id="S2.I1.ix10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal curves, i.e. the image of the forgetful map <math id="S2.I1.ix10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\colon}\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\colon}\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.2 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section will feature <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal will be to prove the independence of the components of the boundary of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> (and consequently <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>). This will be done by a delicate use of test curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">boundary divisor</span> is an irreducible component of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\setminus{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\setminus{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}\setminus{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}\setminus{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.3 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the difficulty in working with the boundary divisors of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> stems from three sources: <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math> There is a very large number of boundary divisors; <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> for degrees <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;5</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction of useful one-parameter families becomes nontrivial; and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> it is often difficult to maintain control over the intersection of test families with boundary divisors. We overcome difficulties <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> by constructing “ramification reducing” and “hyperelliptic” partial pencil families.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before we continue, a technical point: <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not normal. The normalization, which we denote simply by <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g},</annotation></semantics></math> is the smooth Deligne-Mumford stack of twisted stable maps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACV03</a>]</cite>. We will ultimately be concerned primarily with the normalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.4 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the branch morphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Br}\colon\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Br}\colon\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is finite. This means that every boundary divisor <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> will lie over a unique boundary divisor, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Br}(\Delta)\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Br}(\Delta)\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The generic admissible cover <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha\colon C\longrightarrow P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha\colon C\longrightarrow P]</annotation></semantics></math> parametrized by <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> will map to the union of two marked curves: <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{L}\subset{{\bf P}^{1}_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{L}\subset{{\bf P}^{1}_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}\subset{\bf P}^{1}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}\subset{\bf P}^{1}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The curve <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> glued to <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> at a point <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> which is not any of the <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{L}\cup B_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{L}\cup B_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The domain curve <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> breaks up into two halves <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which are the preimages of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a nodal curve, it has a dual graph <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are marked by the geometric genera <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding components. We furthermore label every vertex as either an <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex or an <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex depending on whether it parametrizes a component of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Furthermore, we label every vertex <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with its <span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">degree</span> <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the nodes of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> must lie over the node <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that every edge in <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> must join an <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex with an <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex. Finally, we label every edge <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> with the local degree <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> occurring at the corresponding node.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.5 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We may arrange the vertices of the graph <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of a generic cover <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha\colon C\longrightarrow P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha\colon C\longrightarrow P]</annotation></semantics></math> parametrized by a boundary divisor <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> into two columns, the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">left</span>, and the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">right</span> side. In this way, we associate to every boundary divisor <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> a decorated dual graph, which we call <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can now introduce some useful numerical quantities associated to a boundary divisor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be a boundary component. Then the <span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">ramification index</span> of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the number</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\Delta):=\sum_{\text{all edges $e\in\Gamma_{\Delta}$}}(d_{e}-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mtext>all edges </mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\Delta):=\sum_{\text{all edges $e\in\Gamma_{\Delta}$}}(d_{e}-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a boundary divisor <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">unramified</span> if <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\Delta)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\Delta)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This simple means that the generic admissible cover parameterized by <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> does not have branching at any nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.6 </h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Labeling every vertex and edge of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> will often be notationally cumbersome and unnecessary. Therefore, we will adopt the following convention: Genus <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and unramified edges will usually be left undecorated. Furthermore, we note that since the degree of a vertex <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by all ramification indices of edges incident to <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we need not specify <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">both</span> the degrees of vertices and local degrees of edges – the knowledge of one set of data determines the other.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.7 <math id="S2.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S2.SS7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. </h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The boundary divisors in the space of twisted stable maps <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> lie above the boundary divisors of <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, there is a representable forgetful map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi{\colon}\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi{\colon}\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}^{\rm{adm}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is finite and generically an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will not need a precise method for labeling the boundary divisors in <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, but we will need the following simple lemma which will, in practice, allow us to ignore the distinction between boundary divisors in <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> and boundary divisors in <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a boundary divisor <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a boundary divisor <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> lying over <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f{\colon}S\longrightarrow\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f{\colon}S\longrightarrow\Delta\subset\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth complete curve, there is a finite cover <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}\longrightarrow B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}\longrightarrow B</annotation></semantics></math> which lifts the morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to a morphism <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}{\colon}S^{\prime}\longrightarrow\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}{\colon}S^{\prime}\longrightarrow\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2.7 
          
            
              
              
                ¯
                H
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  d
                  ,
                  g
                
              
            
          
         vs. 
          
            
              
              
                
                
                  ¯
                  H
                
                adm
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  d
                  ,
                  g
                
              
            
          
        . ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>allows us to lift (after finite base change) test families in <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> – thus we will often construct a one parameter family in <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>adm</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{\rm{adm}}</annotation></semantics></math> yet talk about a family in <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained possibly after a base change.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.8 Some examples of boundary divisors.</h3>

<div id="S2.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide some examples for the reader’s convenience. These examples are not selected randomly: they all lie above <math id="S2.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{2}\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{2}\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. two branch points are colliding. In enumerative settings, the divisors lying over <math id="S2.SS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{2}\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{2}\subset[\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{0,b}/\mathfrak{S}_{b}]</annotation></semantics></math> show up most frequently, so we refer to them as the <span id="S2.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">enumeratively relevant</span> boundary divisors. For the reader’s convenience, we indicate the dual graphs of four of the most basic enumeratively relevant divisors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\rm irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>irr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\rm irr}</annotation></semantics></math>, admissible covers having dual graph <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta_{\rm irr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>irr</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta_{\rm irr}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
