<p id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">We will now “concentrate” all of the genus of the vertex <math id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> into a hyperelliptic vertex, which allows us to vary the vertex as in <a href="#S2.SS13.SSS2" title="2.13.2 Hyperelliptic families. ‣ 2.13 Some explicit partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">subsubsection 2.13.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS14.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To accomplish this, we replace this local part of the dual graph with the following picture.</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

