</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">To do this, we use the same type of pencil <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as in <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1.1 
            
              2
            
          -vertex divisors I. ‣ 4.1 Degree three analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, varying the genus <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> component. Fortunately, as is the case for rational partial pencils, the split fiber does not contribute any intersection with further boundary components, because the residual curve <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> curves on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}={\bf P}^{1}\times{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}={\bf P}^{1}\times{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which are blown down as in section <a href="#S2.SS13.SSS1" title="2.13.1 Rational partial pencils. ‣ 2.13 Some explicit partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, the resulting rational partial pencil family has the following intersection numbers:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

