<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">These two dual graphs were dealt with previously. So we already know <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>split</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta_{{\rm ram}},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ram</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta_{{\rm ram}},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now apply <a href="#S4.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 4.13. ‣ 4.1.6 Inequalities III. ‣ 4.1 Degree three analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">4.1.7 <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us suppose <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> has a dual graph with more than <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Then one of the vertices, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, must have degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. it must correspond to a hyperelliptic component. Let us assume <math id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
