</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">At this point, this is routine.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.19</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8g_{R}+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8g_{R}+3</annotation></semantics></math> is explained as follows. A general pencil on <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> hyperelliptic curves has <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8g_{R}+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8g_{R}+4</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. However, we must subtract <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for the gluing section which connects <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">not</span> for the gluing section which connects with the rational node, since this node does not add contribute to <math id="S4.Thmthm19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.20</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the setting of the previous proposition, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta,{{\mathsf{Y}}})\geq g(g_{R}+3)-6g_{R}+c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},{{\mathsf{Y}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>split</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta,{{\mathsf{Y}}})\geq g(g_{R}+3)-6g_{R}+c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},{{\mathsf{Y}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows by intersecting <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=a\lambda-b\delta-{\mathsf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖸</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=a\lambda-b\delta-{\mathsf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and using <a href="#S4.Thmthm18" title="Proposition 4.18. ‣ 4.1.8 
            
              4
            
           vertices. ‣ 4.1 Degree three analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.21</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},Y)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>split</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},Y)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}\leq g_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}\leq g_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expression <math id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(g_{R}+3)-6g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(g_{R}+3)-6g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is easily seen to be nonnegative, so we conclude by <a href="#S4.Thmthm21" title="Corollary 4.21. ‣ 4.1.8 
            
              4
            
           vertices. ‣ 4.1 Degree three analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.21</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">4.2 A simplifying observation.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After seeing the general inductive method in the degree three case, we pause to notice a few things. The underlying pencils used to construct the partial pencils obviously depended on the degree of the vertex <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which was being varied: pencils of trigonal curves in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for degree <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, and pencils of genus <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> hyperelliptic curves in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for degree <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. With the latter hyperelliptic pencils, the quantity <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\delta-a\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\delta-a\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> becomes “much more positive" than it is for pencils of trigonal curves. This simply reflects the fact that the slope <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{8g_{R}+4}{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{8g_{R}+4}{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> of these hyperelliptic families is larger than <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a}{b}=\frac{7g+6}{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a}{b}=\frac{7g+6}{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we are considering the degree four situation, we notice that a similar phenomenon occurs. For a divisor <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> whose dual graph has more than <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, we may use the same types of pencils used in the analysis of lower degree: either pencils of trigonal curves in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the parity of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>), or pencils of hyperelliptic curves of genus <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a}{b}=\frac{13g+15}{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a}{b}=\frac{13g+15}{2g}</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{7g_{R}+6}{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{7g_{R}+6}{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{8g_{R}+4}{g_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{8g_{R}+4}{g_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, inductive arguments exactly like those found in the previous section will show that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for divisors having more than two vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore we arrive at the following simplifying observation:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.Ex51.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_slanted">It is the <math id="S4.Ex51.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex boundary divisors which are of primary interest.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We will need to use the families described in section <a href="#S3.SS11" title="3.11 Constructing families of tetragonal curves. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> to deal with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertex divisors. These same observations hold when we switch to considering pentagonal curves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">4.3 Degree four analysis.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will use the pencils occurring in <a href="#S3.Thmthm18" title="Proposition 3.18. ‣ 3.11.3 ‣ 3.11 Constructing families of tetragonal curves. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.18</span></a>. In order to create partial pencils from these, we will impose a <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of points in a general (conic) fiber of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\bf F}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\bf F}\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to lie in the base locus of our pencil <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">4.3.1 Unramified, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be an unramified, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex divisor with dual graph <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.Ex52.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="29.86" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-15.051833686284938px" version="1.1" viewBox="22.55 15.05 124.11 29.86" width="124.11"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 22.554884515099626 29.857672974663156) translate(0,3.46) translate(22.56,0)"><g transform="translate(-3.46,0) translate(0,-3.07)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.44em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.15)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(-22.56,0) translate(0,7.87) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-1.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.95em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.56" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="13.19"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\scriptstyle v_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\scriptstyle v_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(-22.49,0) translate(0,-7.87) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-1.14)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.94em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.05" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="13.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="l