<div id="S4.Thmthm36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.36</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The partial pencil <math id="S4.Thmthm36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex110"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex110.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex110.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2g_{R}+3-k_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2g_{R}+3-k_{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex111"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex111.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex111.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=31g_{R}+27-7k_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=31g_{R}+27-7k_{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex112"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex112.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex113"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex113.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{{\rm split}}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>split</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{{\rm split}}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex113.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex114"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖷</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex114.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The only number which needs explanation is <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖷</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is written as a combination of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficient of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-22)/5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-22)/5</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we use <a href="#S4.Thmthm34" title="Proposition 4.34. ‣ 4.5.3 ‣ 4.5 A proposition on “rotating directrices.” ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.34</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When we intersect the partial pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with the divisor class <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the following relation:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})=c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},Y)+b(13g_{R}-7k_{R}+27)-a(2g_{R}-k_{R}+3)+((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>split</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})=c(\Delta_{{\rm split}},Y)+b(13g_{R}-7k_{R}+27)-a(2g_{R}-k_{R}+3)+((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.37</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Maintaining the notation above, we have the following inequality:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(13g_{R}-7k_{R}+27)-a(2g_{R}-k_{R}+3)+((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})\geq 3g-\frac{11}{2}g_{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(13g_{R}-7k_{R}+27)-a(2g_{R}-k_{R}+3)+((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})\geq 3g-\frac{11}{2}g_{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is is straightforward - it follows by simplifying the left hand side after replacing <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by the quantity <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{-3(g_{R}+4)}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{-3(g_{R}+4)}{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the inductive procedure carries through as in the degree <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.38</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm38.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm38.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> be as in section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS1" title="4.4.1 Unramified, 
            
              2
            
          -vertex divisors. ‣ 4.4 Degree five analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.1</span></a>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex115" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta,{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that as usual, we assume <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}\leq g_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}\leq g_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that the quantity <math id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3g-\frac{11}{2}g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3g-\frac{11}{2}g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonnegative. We conclude by inductively using <a href="#S4.E14" title="4.14 ‣ 4.5.4 Intersection numbers for the partial pencil 
            
              p
            
          . ‣ 4.5 A proposition on “rotating directrices.” ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 4.14</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">4.5.5 Ramified <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(In this section, we ask the reader to pay attention to the prime in <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.) It remains to establish <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> a ramified, <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex divisor. For this, we do not proceed as in previous cases by imposing nonreduced base loci in the pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}\subset|H_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}\subset|H_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>, since doing so changes the analysis of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we take a different approach. We use the families constructed in section <a href="#S2.SS12.SSS9" title="2.12.9 Variation on the basic construction II. ‣ 2.12 Constructions of partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12.9</span></a> where the surface <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> fibration used in previous sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with a <span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">general</span> pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}\subset|H_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}\subset|H_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> pentagonal curves, and fix a general fiber <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\subset{\bf F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\subset{\bf F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> denote the partial pencil family obtained after performing the necessary degree <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5!</annotation></semantics></math> base change <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:D\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:D\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a branch point of the map <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then recall from section <a href="#S2.SS12.SSS9" title="2.12.9 Variation on the basic construction II. ‣ 2.12 Constructions of partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12.9</span></a> the set <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{z}:=\beta^{-1}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{z}:=\beta^{-1}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. The partial pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a boundary divisor <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and interacts with the higher boundary divisor <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the union of the sets <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{z}\subset D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{z}\subset D</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> varies over all branch points of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of branch points of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows from the Riemann-Hurwitz formula, after recalling that <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an elliptic curve. The sets <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5!/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5!/2</annotation></semantics></math> points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.39</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The family <math id="S4.Thmthm39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> has the following intersection numbers:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex116"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex116.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex116.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=5!\cdot(2g_{R}+3-k_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=5!\cdot(2g_{R}+3-k_{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex117"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex117.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex117.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=5!\cdot(31g_{R}+32-7k_{R})-2400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>31</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2400</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=5!\cdot(31g_{R}+32-7k_{R})-2400</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex118"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex118.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex118.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-1080" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1080</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-1080</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex119"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex119.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta^{\prime}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta^{\prime}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex119.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=600</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex120"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex120.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖷</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathsf{X}}\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex120.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=5!\cdot((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=5!\cdot((2g-22)/5)(k_{R}+m_{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall from section <a href="#S2.SS12.SSS9" title="2.12.9 Variation on the basic construction II. ‣ 2.12 Constructions of partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12.9</span></a> that the base change <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5!</annotation></semantics></math> which is required in order to create the pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. If we let <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=Bl_{B}{\bf F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=Bl_{B}{\bf F}</annotation></semantics></math> be the total space of the pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the base change over <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, then we end up with <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> sections <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1},...,\sigma_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1},...,\sigma_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. (The reader should refer to section <a href="#S2.SS12.SSS9" title="2.12.9 Variation on the basic construction II. ‣ 2.12 Constructions of partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12.9</span></a>.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first note that the sections <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma_{1}+...+\sigma_{5})^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma_{1}+...+\sigma_{5})^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the subset <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, the total intersection of sections, <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i&lt;j}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i&lt;j}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is just the total number of points in <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> varies over the ten branch points of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\longrightarrow{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This totals <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">600</annotation></semantics></math>. Dividing by <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}=600/10=60." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>600</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}=600/10=60.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">From here, we conclude that <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{2}=-120." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{2}=-120.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we must blow up <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> every time it meets another section <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This happens <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 60=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 60=240</annotation></semantics></math> times for a fixed <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, in order to create a family of admissible covers, the section <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must be blown up <span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">twice</span> each time two other sections meet, which happens <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{4\choose 2}\times 60=360" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>360</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{4\choose 2}\times 60=360</annotation></semantics></math> times.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, in the ultimate blown up surface, the proper transform <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> has self intersection <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma_{i}}^{2}=\sigma^{2}-240-720=-1080." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>240</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>720</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1080</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma_{i}}^{2}=\sigma^{2}-240-720=-1080.</annotation></semantics></math> This explains <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, the intersection number <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\cdot p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\cdot p</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated in a similar way: One must take a little bit of care however. Since <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled back from <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we must stabilize the family <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> first, and then compute <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. This has the effect is to not blow up <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> twice at points where two other sections meet. We conclude the calculation of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S3.Thmthm21" title="Proposition 3.21. ‣ 3.12.4 ‣ 3.12 Constructing families of pentagonal curves. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">The calculation of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> follows clearly from <a href="#S3.Thmthm21" title="Proposition 3.21. ‣ 3.12.4 ‣ 3.12 Constructing families of pentagonal curves. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>. The calculation of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the observation that there are <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">600</annotation></semantics></math> points in the union of all sets <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The intersection calculation with the Maroni and Casnati-Ekedahl divisors proceeds as in section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS6" title="4.4.6 Maroni special curves in 
            
              Δ
            
          . ‣ 4.4 Degree five analysis. ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.6</span></a> – at this point we leave these details to the reader.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.40</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm40.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm40.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Maintaining the setting above, we have <math id="S4.Thmthm40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows as usual by intersecting the pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS5.SSS5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{X}}=a\lambda-b\delta-{\mathsf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖸</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{X}}=a\lambda-b\delta-{\mathsf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and using <a href="#S4.Thmthm39" title="Proposition 4.39. ‣ 4.5.5 Ramified 
            
              2
            
          -vertex 
            
              
                
                Δ
                ′
              
            
          . ‣ 4.5 A proposition on “rotating directrices.” ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.39</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">4.5.6 Higher ramification <math id="S4.SS5.SSS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex divisors <math id="S4.SS5.SSS6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis for higher ramification, two vertex divisors proceeds just as in the previous case: we use the same families, except we impose that the pencil <math id="S4.SS5.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has a profile of ramification <math id="S4.SS5.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},...,m_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},...,m_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> as in section <a href="#S2.SS12.SSS10" title="2.12.10 ‣ 2.12 Constructions of partial pencils. ‣ 2 Compactifications. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12.10</span></a>. We leave it to the reader to check using the same strategy as in the proof of <a href="#S4.Thmthm39" title="Proposition 4.39. ‣ 4.5.5 Ramified 
            
              2
            
          -vertex 
            
              
                
                Δ
                ′
              
            
          . ‣ 4.5 A proposition on “rotating directrices.” ‣ 4 The slope problem. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.39</span></a> that the resulting partial pencil family shows that <math id="S4.SS5.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖸</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\Delta^{\prime},{\mathsf{Y}})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">4.5.7 The proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Key result for ). ‣ 3.7 The key result. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> and <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now have all the relevant ingredients for the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Key result for ). ‣ 3.7 The key result. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Key result for ). ‣ 3.7 The key result. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The inequalities presented in this section allow us to conclude <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Key result for ). ‣ 3.7 The key result. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The congruence conditions in <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> precisely correspond to pairs <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,g)</annotation></semantics></math> where both <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> exist as divisors.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, the sweeping one-parameter families achieving the slope bounds are precisely the families found in the three parts of the proof of <a href="#Thmmaintheorem2" title="Theorem B (Rigidity and Extremality). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, since they avoid all higher boundary and do not intersect <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">follows immediately from <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Key result for ). ‣ 3.7 The key result. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, which we just proved. One point of care: The statement of <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> involves the standard compactification of twisted stable maps <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have technically only proven the result for the spaces <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The modification for the proof in the case of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is simple: There is a stabilization map <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}\longrightarrow\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> which is finite and bijective on points. Thus, the coefficients of <math id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> in both spaces have the same sign.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm41" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm41.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.41</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm41.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm41.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm41.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm41.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The slope bounds in <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> also apply to the respective loci <math id="S4.Thmthm41.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{d,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{d,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.8 The locus 
          
            
              
              
                
                M
                1
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  d
                  ,
                  g ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Further directions and open questions.</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our purpose in this paper was to introduce the relevance of the loci of covers possessing atypicial behaviour in their Casnati-Ekedahl resolutions. Many questions are left unanswered, and we present some which intrigue us in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}:=\{\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid\text{${\mathcal{N}}_{i}$ is unbalanced}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> is unbalanced</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}:=\{\alpha\in{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\mid\text{${\mathcal{N}}_{i}$ is unbalanced}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generic balancedness)<span id="S5.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> a proper subset of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned already, the question is already interesting for the bundle of quadrics <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The next question concerns the expected dimension of the locus <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Expected dimension)<span id="S5.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Are the loci <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of expected dimension? When is <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> divisorial?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We do not know the answer to <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Question 5.2 (Expected dimension). ‣ 5 Further directions and open questions. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> even in the low degree regime. Specifically, we know all Casnati-Ekedahl loci have expected dimension when <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math> (as a simple consequence of the <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">trigonal construction</span>), but we do not know this beginning with <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Which bundles <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are realized in the Casnati-Ekedahl resolution of a cover?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This question seems extremely difficult. The general problem of determining which invariants are realizable by a cover is already wide open for the bundle <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same holds for the Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is it possible to contract the Maroni divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> or the Casnati-Ekedahl divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A candidate for the contraction of <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a GIT quotient of a Hilbet scheme. The Maroni divisor <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists precisely when the scroll <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a general branched cover is trivial, i.e. <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}={\bf P}^{d-2}\times{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}{\mathcal{E}}={\bf P}^{d-2}\times{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We let <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closure of the scheme of relative canonical at-worst-nodal curves in <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-1}\times{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-1}\times{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree genus <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subscheme of the appropriate Hilbert scheme. Let <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G={\rm PGL}_{d-1}(k)\times{\rm PGL}_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G={\rm PGL}_{d-1}(k)\times{\rm PGL}_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">There is then a birational map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi:\overline{H}_{d,g}\dashrightarrow V_{d,g}//G" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\overline{H}_{d,g}\dashrightarrow V_{d,g}//G</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where the latter space is a GIT quotient. (It is unclear what linearization we would want.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In all understood cases, which is not many, a aparameter count suggests that the Maroni divisor <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> experiences a contraction under <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> were a regular morphism, a contraction of <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> would be enough to conclude rigidity and extremality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">However, since <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is most probably not a morphism, we would need to more carefully analyze the resolution of its indeterminacy (which is contained in <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>). This analysis currently seems somewhat daunting, even in the case <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Question 5.4. ‣ 5 Further directions and open questions. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>is related to establishing the rigidity of the respective loci. In fact, it could be the case that some multiple of the Casnati-Ekedahl divisor <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> may not be rigid. Together with Anand Deopurkar, the author has computed the Bogomolov expressions of the higher syzygy bundles, and the result is surprising: All Bogomolov expressions are multiples of the divisor class of <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As pointed out to the author by Deopurkar, this is very similar to the story of the Brill-Noether divisors in <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming the supports of the divisorial loci <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct, we would conclude that some multiple of <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathsf{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathsf{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> would not be rigid. This does not rule out extremality, however.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Even in the low degree regime, interesting questions remain. Recall that when <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and the genus <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, there is the <span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">tangency divisor</span> <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Tan}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tan</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Tan}</annotation></semantics></math> which generically parmaterizes covers which are tangent to the directrix in <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This divisor is extremal, and no multiple of it deforms, as can be proved using the same technique as in the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3.10 Rigidity and Extremality of 
          
            M
          
         for trigonal curves. ‣ 3 One parameter families and . ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>. It also plays the key role in the slope problem for trigonal curves of odd genus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are the higher degree analogues of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Tan}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tan</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Tan}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">More generally,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Effective cone)<span id="S5.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are all extremal rays of the effective cone <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Eff}\,\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Eff</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Eff}\,\overline{{\mathcal{H}}}_{d,g}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">We do not know the answer to this question even when <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, although we suspect that the Maroni divisor or the tangency divisor, along with boundary components generate the effective cone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">One consequence of <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> is that the pentagonal locus <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}\subset{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be swept out by <math id="S5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}}</annotation></semantics></math>–negative curves, because such a curve would have slope <math id="S5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 6\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⁤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 6\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> does not allow for this. (Of course we are only considering the appropriate genera occurring in <a href="#Thmmaintheorem3" title="Theorem C (Slope bounds). ‣ 0.2 Outline and main results. ‣ Special Codimension One Loci in Hurwitz Spaces" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, one cannot immediately conclude from this that <math id="S5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math> does not lie in the base locus of the canonical divisor <math id="S5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but we still ask the question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does the pentagonal locus <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{1}_{5,g}</annotation></semantics></math> play a fundamentally different role from the trigonal or tetragonal locus in the log MMP program for <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, one can ask about generalizing the ideas in this paper to Hurwitz spaces of covers of higher genus curves. In a forthcoming paper with Gabriel Bujokas, we will investigate the analogues of Maroni and Casnati-Ekedahl divisors in spaces of branched covers of elliptic curves.</p>
</div>
</section>
